from __future__ import annotations

from thinking_core import render_long


def coe_not_ai_department() -> str:
    body = '''<p>An AI CoE often starts for good reasons.</p><p>Expertise is scarce. Governance is immature. Business teams need help translating opportunities into something investable. Architecture, data, risk and delivery practices are still forming.</p><p>Centralizing capability creates speed and consistency.</p><p>The problem begins when temporary concentration becomes permanent dependency.</p><p>Business teams bring the problem. The CoE interprets it, finds the expertise, coordinates delivery, manages the governance questions and stays involved long enough to keep the solution operating.</p><p>Demand for the CoE may be increasing, yet the enterprise itself is not becoming much more capable.</p><blockquote>A strong AI CoE should make the organization more capable of owning AI — not more dependent on the CoE to do AI for it.</blockquote><h2>Central expertise and business ownership are different things</h2><p>There are capabilities I would deliberately centralize.</p><p>Architecture patterns, governance methods, specialist expertise, reusable components, common tooling, literacy and enterprise portfolio visibility all benefit from consistency.</p><p>Having every business function reinvent those foundations would create unnecessary cost, duplication and risk.</p><p>But business accountability belongs somewhere else.</p><p>If an AI use case changes customer service, warranty decisions, sales activity, planning or another operational process, the accountable owner should sit close to that outcome.</p><p>The CoE can provide the system, expertise and guardrails.</p><p>It should not inherit the business result simply because it helped build the solution.</p><blockquote>Centralize what must be reusable. Distribute what must be owned.</blockquote><h2>The real choice is not centralized or decentralized</h2><p>I do not see those as the only two options.</p><p>A fully centralized model gives consistency, but it can create queues, distance from the business and weak ownership.</p><p>A fully distributed model gets closer to the problem, but it can duplicate expertise, fragment architecture and make governance inconsistent.</p><p>The more useful question is:</p><blockquote>What should remain common, what should sit with the business, and when should ownership move?</blockquote><p>That usually leads me toward a federated model.</p><p>The enterprise capability provides patterns, specialist support, governance, portfolio visibility and reusable foundations. Business or product teams own priorities, workflow change, adoption and outcomes.</p><p>The hand-off between those responsibilities needs to be explicit.</p><h2>What I expect an AI CoE to own</h2><p>For me, five responsibilities justify a central capability.</p><p><strong>1. A common lifecycle.</strong> Teams need a clear route from opportunity to evidence, investment, production and review without forcing every use case through identical controls.</p><p><strong>2. Clear decision rights.</strong> Who can approve experimentation? Who owns value? When is central risk review required? Who can stop or escalate an initiative?</p><p><strong>3. Reusable capability.</strong> Architecture, evaluation methods, governance patterns, data practices, literacy and shared components should make the next use case easier than the previous one.</p><p><strong>4. Enterprise portfolio visibility.</strong> Leadership needs to see where investment is going, where duplication exists, which assumptions remain weak and where attention should increase or stop.</p><p><strong>5. Capability transfer.</strong> The center should deliberately make business and product teams more able to own AI responsibly themselves.</p><p>That fifth responsibility is the one I think organizations underestimate.</p><h2>A mature CoE should become less necessary for routine AI work</h2><p>This can be uncomfortable because central teams are often measured by how much they deliver.</p><p>I would measure something different as well.</p><p>Are business owners becoming stronger?</p><p>Are routine decisions happening without central intervention?</p><p>Are teams reusing common patterns instead of starting again?</p><p>Is the route from idea to decision becoming shorter?</p><p>Can more teams operate AI responsibly without continuous support?</p><p>If the answers improve, the CoE is scaling capability.</p><p>If the center remains the permanent owner of ordinary AI delivery, it may be scaling its own workload instead.</p><p>The CoE should remain important for enterprise standards, novel problems, higher-risk work, specialist depth and portfolio governance.</p><p>But success should eventually mean that it is needed <strong>less</strong> for the ordinary work.</p><div class="rule"><strong>Build the AI CoE as an enabling system, not as the enterprise AI delivery department.</strong></div>'''
    aside = '''<span>CoE design test</span><strong>What should remain central — and what should move?</strong><div class="article-questions"><div>Common lifecycle, standards and portfolio visibility</div><div>Reusable architecture, governance and specialist capability</div><div>Business ownership of priorities, workflow change and outcomes</div><div>Explicit hand-offs between central and business teams</div><div>Less central intervention as ordinary AI work matures</div></div><p>A strong CoE scales enterprise capability, not dependency on the center.</p>'''
    return render_long("coe_not_ai_department", body, aside)


def strategy_to_value_framework() -> str:
    body = '''<p>Organizations rarely lack AI activity.</p><p>There are pilots, use cases, governance discussions, platforms, training programs and executive ambition. Yet the business impact can still be difficult to see.</p><p>When that happens, my first reaction is not necessarily to accelerate delivery.</p><p>I want to know where the management chain has broken.</p><p>The framework I use is deliberately simple:</p><div class="flow" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(5,1fr);margin:32px 0"><div class="step"><span>01</span><strong>Strategy</strong></div><div class="step"><span>02</span><strong>Portfolio</strong></div><div class="step"><span>03</span><strong>Governance</strong></div><div class="step"><span>04</span><strong>Adoption</strong></div><div class="step"><span>05</span><strong>Value</strong></div></div><p>Each link answers a different leadership question. If one is weak, adding more AI activity often makes the problem larger rather than solving it.</p><h2>Strategy — where should AI matter?</h2><p>AI strategy should begin with business priorities, not with a catalogue of technologies.</p><p>The useful question is not simply where generative AI, agents, prediction or automation could be used.</p><p>It is:</p><blockquote>Where could AI materially change an outcome the organization already cares about?</blockquote><p>That creates direction.</p><p>Without it, the enterprise may generate many credible use cases but struggle to explain why some deserve attention more than others.</p><h2>Portfolio — what deserves investment now?</h2><p>Strategy creates possibilities. Portfolio management creates choices.</p><p>No organization can responsibly pursue every promising AI opportunity at the same time.</p><p>The portfolio should therefore expose assumptions, sequence investments, fund learning where uncertainty matters and stop work that no longer deserves capacity.</p><p>For me, the central question is:</p><blockquote>Which opportunity deserves the next unit of investment relative to the alternatives?</blockquote><p>If everything remains a priority, the strategy has not yet become an investment system.</p><h2>Governance — what must be true before we increase the commitment?</h2><p>Governance should create confidence, not unnecessary friction.</p><p>I do not want every experiment facing the same controls. I do want ownership, evidence, data responsibilities, risk and escalation to become clearer as the potential consequence of the use case increases.</p><p>That means governance should evolve with the investment.</p><p>The question is:</p><blockquote>What evidence and accountability must exist before this use case gets more reach, more authority or more money?</blockquote><p>Good governance allows the enterprise to move faster because the conditions for responsible decisions are explicit.</p><h2>Adoption — what actually changed in the work?</h2><p>Deployment is not adoption.</p><p>Usage is not adoption either.</p><p>I want to know what changed in the workflow, decision, role or behaviour because the AI capability exists.</p><p>Did a step disappear?</p><p>Did a decision become faster or more consistent?</p><p>Did employees stop using the previous workaround?</p><p>Did the new behaviour persist after the launch campaign?</p><p>Without that change, the organization may have implemented technology without changing operations.</p><h2>Value — did the changed work improve an outcome?</h2><p>Value is the last link in the chain, but it cannot be an afterthought.</p><p>Before major investment, there should already be a credible mechanism connecting the AI capability to changed work and changed work to a measurable outcome.</p><p>The chain might look like:</p><blockquote>AI capability → workflow change → operational effect → business outcome</blockquote><p>If that relationship is vague, calculating ROI later will not repair the problem.</p><h2>I use the framework as a diagnostic, not a sequence</h2><p>These five elements are connected, but they are not a waterfall.</p><p>A portfolio decision can expose a weak strategy.</p><p>Governance can reveal that nobody really owns the outcome.</p><p>Adoption problems can show that the use case was designed around the technology rather than the workflow.</p><p>Weak value evidence can change future portfolio priorities.</p><p>That is why, when an AI initiative is stuck, I prefer to identify the weakest link before adding more delivery pressure.</p><p>Sometimes the problem is technology.</p><p>But often it is somewhere else in the operating system.</p><div class="rule"><strong>Strategy gives direction. Portfolio creates choice. Governance creates confidence. Adoption changes work. Value proves the outcome.</strong><p>The leadership discipline is keeping all five connected.</p></div>'''
    aside = '''<span>Five-link diagnostic</span><strong>When AI activity is high but impact is weak, find the broken link.</strong><div class="article-questions"><div><strong>Strategy:</strong> Where should AI materially change a business outcome?</div><div><strong>Portfolio:</strong> What deserves the next unit of investment?</div><div><strong>Governance:</strong> What evidence and accountability are required?</div><div><strong>Adoption:</strong> What actually changed in the work?</div><div><strong>Value:</strong> Did the changed work improve an outcome?</div></div><p>Use the chain as a management diagnostic before adding more delivery pressure.</p>'''
    return render_long("strategy_to_value_framework", body, aside)
